Example:
import torch

@torch.jit.script
def neg():
    x = -1.0
    return x

print(neg.graph)

python2:
graph() {
  %x : Float() = prim::Constant[value={-1}]()
  return (%x);
}

python3:
graph() {
  %0 : Float() = prim::Constant[value={1}]()
  %x : Dynamic = aten::neg(%0)
  return (%x);
}

This is due to the way that negative number literals are represented in Python's AST between versions. Ideas for fixes:

Emit the UnaryOp for both
Constant propagation

Issue description
Correct me if I am wrong but it seems that there is a needless unordered_set filled from the observers_list_ just to check for parameter duplicate. I think this set can be removed completely and the check can be done in the filling loop.
Code example
observer.h:52
std::unordered_set<const Observer*> observers;
for (auto& ob : observers_list_) {
  observers.insert(ob.get());
}
const auto* observer_ptr = observer.get();
if (observers.count(observer_ptr)) {
  return observer_ptr;
}
observers_list_.push_back(std::move(observer));
return observer_ptr;

the code above should be changed to something like:
const auto* observer_ptr = observer.get();
for (auto& ob : observers_list_) {
   if (ob.get() == observer_ptr)
       return observer_ptr;
}
observers_list_.push_back(std::move(observer));
return observer_ptr;

With
class Test(torch.nn.Module):
    def forward(self, x):
        return x.transpose(1, 2).transpose(0, 1)

we have
>>> torch.onnx.export(Test(), torch.rand(2, 3, 5), 'test.onnx', verbose=True)
graph(%0 : Float(2, 3, 5)) {
  %1 : Float(5!, 2, 3!) = onnx::Transpose[perm=[1, 2, 0]](%0), scope: Test
  return (%1);
}

when we expect Transpose[perm=[2, 0, 1]](%0) or an equivalent.
So one of the transformations in https://github.com/pytorch/pytorch/blob/master/torch/csrc/jit/passes/onnx/peephole.cpp is wrong.
cc @ryanleary

Code example:
import torch

@torch.jit.script
def capture_bug(x):
    output = 0
    for i in range(20):
        if i == 0:
            output = x.unsqueeze(0)
        else:
            output = torch.cat((output, x.unsqueeze(0)), dim=0)
    return output

print(capture_bug.graph)

Creates:
graph(%x : Dynamic) {
  %output.1 : Long() = prim::Constant[value={0}]()
  %2 : Long() = prim::Constant[value={20}]()
  %3 : Byte() = prim::Constant[value={1}]()
  %output : Dynamic = prim::Loop(%2, %3, %output.1)
    block0(%i : Dynamic, %15 : Dynamic) {
      %5 : Long() = prim::Constant[value={0}]()
      %6 : Dynamic = aten::eq(%i, %5)
      %output.4 : Dynamic = prim::If(%6)
        block0() {
          %output.2 : Dynamic = aten::unsqueeze[dim=0](%x)
          -> (%output.2)
        }
        block1() {
          %10 : Dynamic = aten::unsqueeze[dim=0](%x)
          %12 : Long() = prim::Constant[value={0}]()
          %output.3 : Dynamic = aten::cat(%output.1, %10, %12)
          -> (%output.3)
        }
      %16 : Byte() = prim::Constant[value={1}]()
      -> (%16, %output.4)
    }
  return (%output);
}

The first operand to cat in block1() should be a captured value, not %output.1

To juggle CUDA streams from C++ I need to call THCState_getCurrentStreamOnDevice and friends. It would be nice to have an ATen wrapper for this to not have to include files from TH*.
The ATen include tree doesn't expose anything CUDA specific though, so some discussion is warranted before adding a wrapper. What would be the right place such a wrapper?
